WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.481
Before this service takes traffic, three caches must be warm.

2
00:00:06.481 --> 00:00:10.137
The thread that opens the port must wait for three loaders.

3
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:13.113
A CountDownLatch is a gate with a counter.

4
00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:14.355
It starts at three.

5
00:00:14.355 --> 00:00:17.363
The opener calls await, and stops at the gate.

6
00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:20.392
Each loader calls countDown when it is done.

7
00:00:20.392 --> 00:00:21.420
Three, two, one.

8
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.667
At zero, the gate opens, and every thread waiting there goes on.

9
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.201
Now a late thread calls await.

10
00:00:29.201 --> 00:00:30.199
Does it wait?

11
00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:31.994
No.

12
00:00:31.994 --> 00:00:33.509
The gate stays open.

13
00:00:33.509 --> 00:00:37.447
The count never goes back up, and there is no reset.

14
00:00:37.447 --> 00:00:38.961
A latch is one-shot.

15
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:41.976
Next, four workers simulate one day each.

16
00:00:41.976 --> 00:00:45.196
Nobody may start day two until all four finish.

17
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.053
A CyclicBarrier counts threads, not events.

18
00:00:48.053 --> 00:00:51.323
Each worker calls await at the line, and waits there.

19
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:56.402
The fourth to arrive runs the barrier action once, here adding up the day.

20
00:00:56.402 --> 00:00:58.309
Then all four go on together.

21
00:00:58.533 --> 00:00:59.113
Day two.

22
00:00:59.113 --> 00:01:01.939
Is the gate still open, like the latch?

23
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:03.757
No.

24
00:01:03.757 --> 00:01:05.846
It closed again behind them.

25
00:01:05.846 --> 00:01:08.681
The barrier resets itself every round.

26
00:01:08.681 --> 00:01:10.472
That is the cyclic part.

27
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.566
A Phaser is a barrier whose group can change.

28
00:01:13.566 --> 00:01:15.667
A party can deregister and leave.

29
00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:19.488
Each phase then waits only for the parties still registered.

30
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.146
Last, the card issuer allows 20 calls at once.

31
00:01:23.146 --> 00:01:25.093
A Semaphore holds permits.

32
00:01:25.093 --> 00:01:25.992
Here, three.

33
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.669
acquire takes a permit.

34
00:01:27.669 --> 00:01:30.544
Only a thread with a permit calls the issuer.

35
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:33.995
The fourth thread finds the tray empty, so it waits.

36
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.007
When a call finishes, release gives the permit back, and the waiting thread takes it.

37
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:42.726
Now a thread that never took a permit calls release.

38
00:01:42.726 --> 00:01:43.599
What happens?

39
00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:47.361
The semaphore just adds a permit.

40
00:01:47.361 --> 00:01:48.823
Permits have no owner.

41
00:01:48.823 --> 00:01:52.547
Now four threads fit, and the limit has quietly drifted.

42
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:57.046
Side by side: a latch waits for events, once.

43
00:01:57.046 --> 00:01:59.761
A barrier waits for a group, every round.

44
00:01:59.761 --> 00:02:02.343
A semaphore limits how many are inside.

45
00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:07.427
In code, each is one constructor, in Java and in Kotlin.

46
00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:12.419
The rule: count events with a latch, meet with a barrier, limit with a semaphore.

47
00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:14.917
Quick check.

48
00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:17.725
Four workers settle twelve monthly batches.

49
00:02:17.725 --> 00:02:20.730
Each month must finish before the next starts.

50
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:21.840
Latch or barrier?

51
00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:24.718
A barrier.

52
00:02:24.718 --> 00:02:27.865
A latch would stay open after the first month.
